    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d810">

                <pb facs="0916" n="804" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg804"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.52/338</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 3340</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-02-06">February 6, 1935</date>.<lb/>[Received March
                        11.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the Legation’s
                    despatch No. 3282 of January 18, 1935, on the subject of the reregistration of
                    land titles at Nanking, and to enclose a copy of despatch No. 739 of January 29,
                    1935, from the Consulate General at Nanking<anchor n="31" xml:id="d810-f1"/>
                    reporting further developments in the matter. There is also enclosed a copy of
                    the Legation’s instruction of today’s date in reply.<anchor n="31"
                        xml:id="d810-f2"/></p>
                <pb facs="0917" n="805" xml:id="pg805"/>
                <p>The Department will note that the American missions have received fairly
                    satisfactory assurances from the Municipal Government in regard to the points at
                    issue, and that the Legation has instructed Mr. Peck<anchor n="32"
                        xml:id="d810-f3"/> that, should the new land certificates which he states
                    are now in the course of revision, contain any restrictions inimical to existing
                    property rights he should immediately file a protest with the Municipal
                    Government and reserve treaty rights for the American missions concerned.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">C. E. Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
